&lt;p&gt;Have you ever stopped to think that just one hour of your time could help save a life?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Donating blood is one of the clearest and easiest ways to make an immediate difference in the world. A simple act of generosity can become someone else&amp;#39;s second chance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each year, Columbia&amp;#39;s&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Communities that Care&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Summer Blood Drive&lt;/strong&gt; helps ensure that hospitals across our region have the lifesaving blood they need when patients need it most. Blood donations are essential for people undergoing cancer treatment, emergency surgeries, organ transplants, and countless other medical procedures. A single car crash victim, for example, can require as many as 100 units of blood.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you are eligible, I hope you&amp;#39;ll consider joining us.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How has America&amp;rsquo;s relationship with its armed forces evolved over the past 250 years? From Lexington and Concord to Kabul and the Caribbean, America&amp;rsquo;s military has played important roles in the nation&amp;rsquo;s history. This seminar will consider how those roles have and have not changed from the eighteenth-century to today. We&amp;rsquo;ll look at the Founders&amp;rsquo; anxieties about standing armies, consider the role of the armed forces in Reconstruction, examine military support for integration at home, and ask what the tasks the military might find itself asked to perform in the coming years. This summer, we will meet three times for discussion seminars, followed by a fourth meeting that will serve as a launching pad for a conversation about the future. At stake: what should America&amp;rsquo;s relationship with its armed forces look like in the 21&lt;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;century?&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb7c-9ebae55f-019e-bd562be1-0000432bevents@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId="&gt;Wed, Jul&amp;nbsp;8, 5:30&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; 7pm&lt;/a&gt;: Introductions; America&amp;rsquo;s Sense of Its Own Military&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb7c-9ebae55f-019e-bd5ef2d2-00004423events@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId="&gt;Wed, Jul&amp;nbsp;15, 5:30&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; 7pm&lt;/a&gt;: The Armed Forces Abroad&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb7c-9ebae55f-019e-bd62c99b-000044a1events@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId="&gt;Wed, Jul&amp;nbsp;22, 5:30&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; 7pm&lt;/a&gt;:&amp;nbsp;The Military at Home&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
